  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Provision of emergency and transitional shelter for vulnerable IDPs in the Tabalah and Alkhazzan camps, Al-Shahir District, Hadramout Governorate.</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">ltpgtltbrgtIn Alshihr district, Hadhramout Governorate, recurrent floods, cyclones, a&#xa0;deteriorating economic situation, and prolonged displacement have disrupted access to safe shelter, clean water, and livelihoods for IDPs and host communities. Per the 2025 Humanitarian Needs Response Plan (HNRP),&#xa0;10,434 individuals (2,343 women, 2,784 girls, 2,382 men, 2,925 boys) live in catastrophic shelter conditions. In Tabalah (formerly Samoun) and Alkhazzan IDP camps, old, overcrowded shelters&#x2014;vulnerable to floods, cyclones, and heat&#x2014;heighten health and protection risks, especially for women, the elderly, and children. Leveraging its strong presence since 2017 and expertise in shelter, BCHR proposes a shelter&#xa0;project to address these challenges.lt/pgtltpgtltbrgtThe project aims to enhance living conditions and resilience for the severely affected households in Tabalah and Alkhazzan IDP camps&#xa0;in Alshahr, through provision of the Emergency shelter kits and translational shelter. It seeks to reduce immediate risks&#x2014;disease, GBV, and exposure&#x2014;while fostering long-term stability with integrated solutions.lt/pgtltpgtltbrgtIn Tabalah and Alkhazzan IDP camps, the project targets shelter needs for&#xa0;120&#xa0;of the most affected IDP HHs (600 individuals&#xa0;135 women, 160 girls, 137 men, and 168 boys), distributing Emergency Shelter Kits (ESKs) to 100 households for temporary relief and building transitional shelters for 20 of the most vulnerable, featuring durable, climate-resilient designs (e.g., flood-proof, insulated), improving safety and dignity.&#xa0;lt/pgt</narrative>
  </description>
